1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,000
Welcome back.

2
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Clarity is where reliability lives in a Cloud application.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Get the instructions wrong, and you'll

4
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,000
chase flaky output with sampling knobs

5
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,000
that were never the problem.

6
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:13,000
This lesson covers how to write instructions

7
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Cloud follows the same way every single time.

8
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
And the tell that you're asked, not the model, is at fault. Let's dive in.

9
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Let's start with what a clear instruction actually looks like.

10
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
And it comes down to three marks.

11
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,000
First, specific.

12
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
You state exactly what you want in concrete terms,

13
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
the output format, the fields you need,

14
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
the limits to respect, instead of gesturing

15
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
at some general goal.

16
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Second, unambiguous.

17
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,000
There's a golden rule here I love.

18
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Show your prompt to a colleague who

19
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,000
has little context on the task, and ask them to follow it.

20
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:54,000
If they'd be confused, Cloud will be too. Third, ordered.

21
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,000
When the sequence matters, or you need every step done,

22
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
lay the steps out as a numbered list

23
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
so Cloud works through them in the order you intended.

24
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Specific, unambiguous, ordered.

25
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Hit those three, and most output problems just disappear.

26
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Here's a mental model that makes all of this click.

27
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Think of Cloud as a brilliant but brand new employee,

28
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,000
one who's genuinely sharp but has

29
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,000
zero context on your norms, your workflows,

30
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,000
or how your team likes things done.

31
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
You wouldn't hand a new hire a one-line request

32
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,000
and expect perfect judgment on everything you left unsaid. Same here.

33
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Spell out the conventions you'd explain on someone's first week.

34
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Don't assume shared jargon or a default house style

35
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Cloud has never seen.

36
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
And if you want it to go above and beyond a literal reading

37
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,000
of the task, say so directly, because a careful new employee

38
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:52,000
tends to do exactly what's written.

39
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Give the context a newcomer would need

40
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
and the quality climbs.

41
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Let me make this concrete with a before and after.

42
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
The vague ask is, summarize this ticket.

43
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Read it the way Cloud has to. How long?

44
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
One line, one paragraph. What tone?

45
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Who's the reader?

46
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Which details matter?

47
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:14,000
None of that is stated, so the model

48
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,000
fills the gaps differently each time, and two runs rarely match.

49
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Now the specific ask, summarize this ticket in two sentences

50
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,000
for an on-call engineer and list the affected service.

51
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Look at what changed.

52
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
The length is fixed, the audience is named,

53
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
and the required field is called out.

54
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Every run now lands in roughly the same shape

55
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
because you removed the guesswork.

56
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Specific beats vague, not because it's longer,

57
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,000
but because it closes the gaps.

58
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
One of the most reliable format levers

59
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:49,000
is almost counterintuitive.

60
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Tell Cloud what to do, not what not to do.

61
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Say you want smoothly flowing prose.

62
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,000
The weak version is, don't use markdown,

63
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
don't use bullet points, don't use headers.

64
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:01,000
You're describing a hole and hoping

65
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
the model lands outside it.

66
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
The strong version just names the target.

67
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Your response should be composed of flowing prose paragraphs.

68
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Now Cloud has something positive to aim at,

69
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
so walk through your prohibitions

70
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
and convert each one into the matching do.

71
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Keep negatives only for genuine hard limits,

72
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:23,000
the real red lines.

73
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
As a rule, describing the behavior you want

74
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
steers the output far more reliably

75
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,000
than listing everything you want it to avoid.

76
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Beyond clear prose, you have three concrete levers

77
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,000
for steering output format.

78
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,000
The first is positive framing, which we just covered.

79
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Name the behavior you want instead

80
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
of stacking up prohibitions.

81
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:47,000
The second is XML tags.

82
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:50,000
You can ask Cloud to place its answer inside named tags.

83
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Something like, write your summary

84
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,000
inside prose paragraph tags.

85
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:58,000
Tags give the model an unmistakable container,

86
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
and they make the output trivial to pull out on your end.

87
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
The third is matching your prompt style

88
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:05,000
to the output you want.

89
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:10,000
If you write the prompt itself in clean, markdown-free prose,

90
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Cloud tends to answer in kind because it

91
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
mirrors the register it sees.

92
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Positive framing, tags, matching style,

93
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
three cheap levers you can reach for before anything heavier.

94
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
Here's a technique that punches way above its weight.

95
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Add the reason behind a rule, not just the rule.

96
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Compare two versions.

97
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,000
The bare command is, never use ellipses.

98
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
The version with context is, your response

99
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
will be read aloud by a text-to-speech engine,

100
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:41,000
so never use ellipses.

101
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:43,000
That second one works better, and the reason

102
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:45,000
is generalization.

103
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
When Cloud understands why a rule exists,

104
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
it can extend the intent to situations

105
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
you never explicitly named.

106
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
A bare rule only covers the one case you spelled out.

107
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
A rule with its motivation covers the whole family

108
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:01,000
of cases the motivation implies.

109
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:02,000
There's a bonus, too.

110
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,000
The why documents your intent for the next developer

111
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:07,000
who reads the prompt.

112
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Let's turn all of this into a checklist

113
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,000
you can run on any instruction.

114
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,000
Three ways to sharpen an ask.

115
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:16,000
First, name the format.

116
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:19,000
Do you want JSON, prose, a table, a list?

117
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,000
And if it's structured, name the exact fields you expect back.

118
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Don't make Cloud guess the shape.

119
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Second, bound the length.

120
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Give a concrete limit, two sentences, five bullets,

121
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:36,000
a word cap, so responses stay consistent instead of quietly

122
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:38,000
drifting longer over time.

123
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Third, sequence the steps.

124
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,000
When the task has several parts, hand over a numbered list

125
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,000
so nothing gets skipped and the order you care about actually holds.

126
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:53,000
Name the format, bound the length, sequence the steps.

127
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Run those three checks, and a loose request

128
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,000
becomes a precise one.

129
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:02,000
Let's close this lesson with the habit that ties it together.

130
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
When your output comes back inconsistent,

131
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,000
resist the urge to blame the model

132
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
or start twisting sampling settings.

133
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Almost always, the ask itself is vague or contradictory,

134
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
and that's where you look first.

135
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:15,000
So reread your instruction as if you

136
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:19,000
knew nothing about the goal the way that new colleague would.

137
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:21,000
Where could it be read more than one way?

138
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Then tighten the wording and the constraints

139
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
before you reach for sampling knobs at all,

140
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,000
which current models no longer accept.

141
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
And only after the instruction is genuinely clear

142
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:34,000
should you reach for examples, because examples

143
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:38,000
layered on a muddy ask just teach the model your confusion.

144
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:39,000
Fix the instruction first.

145
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:42,000
It's the cheapest move you have.

146
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:46,000
Clear instructions do the heavy lifting for output reliability

147
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
because they close the gaps a model would otherwise

148
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:50,000
fill on its own.

149
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
Just remember, when output drifts,

150
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
tighten the ask before you reach for sampling.

151
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,000
That covers writing clear, unambiguous instructions.

152
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
I'll see you in the next one.

